      "math_academy_id": "e-14422",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u = \\ln x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u=\\dfrac 1 x\\,{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n&= \\int_{e}^{e^2} (\\ln x)^2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{1}^{2} u^2\\,\\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n&=\\left.\\dfrac13u^{3}\\right|_{1}^{2}\\[5pt] \n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(2^3-1^3 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(8-1 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{7}{3}\t\t\n\n\t}\n$$",
